        "subtitle": "Blue, a primary colour, has been the hardest colour for artists and scientists to create.",
        "text": "Artists have always been enchanted by blue, but fine blues have long been hard to obtain. Blues are fairly rare in nature, so painters through the ages have found themselves at the mercy of whatever the chemical technology of their day could offer. Some blues have been extremely expensive; others were unreliable. The search for a good blue has driven some crucial technological innovations, which shows that the relationship between art and science has not always been one-way.\n\nThe first pigments were simply coloured minerals dug from the earth and ground up. But few blue minerals make suitable pigments, so there are no blues in cave art. Ancient Egyptian artists, however, used blue a great deal, because they knew how to make a fine artificial pigment, now known as Egyptian blue.\n\nLike many other artificial pigments, Egyptian blue was almost certainly discovered by accident. The Egyptians manufactured blue-glazed stones and ornaments called faience, using a technique they had inherited from the Mesopotamians. Making faience was big business in the ancient world: it was traded all over Europe by 1500 BC. Faience is made by heating stone ornaments in a kiln with copper minerals such as malachite. Egyptian blue, which was made from at least 2500 BC, comes from firing chalk or limestone with sand and copper minerals, and it probably appeared by the chance mixture of these ingredients in a faience kiln.\n\nScientists have recently worked out the secrets of another ancient pigment: Maya blue, which was used for centuries throughout Central America before the Spanish Conquest. It is a kind of clay (a mineral made of sheets of atoms) with molecules of the blue dye indigo wedged between the sheets. Using indigo in this way makes it less likely to decompose. Nobody has made colours this way since the Maya, and no one knows exactly how they did it. But technologists are now interested in using the same trick to make stable pigments from other dyes.\n\nThe finest pigment available to medieval artists was ultramarine, which began to appear in Western art in the 13th century. It was made from the blue mineral lapis lazuli, and only one source of it was known: the remote mines of Badakhshan, in what is now Afghanistan. Besides the difficulty of transporting the mineral over such distances, making the pigment was extremely hard work. Lapis lazuli turns greyish when it is powdered, because of impurities in the mineral. To extract the pure blue pigment, the powder has to be mixed into a dough with wax and kneaded again and again in water.\n\nAs a result, ultramarine could cost more than its weight in gold, and medieval artists used it very selectively. Since the Renaissance, painters had longed for a cheaper, more accessible blue to rival ultramarine. Things improved in 1704, when a Berlin colour maker called Diesbach discovered the first \"modern\" synthetic pigment: Prussian blue. Diesbach was trying to make a red pigment with a recipe that involved the alkali potash. But his potash was contaminated with animal oil, and the synthesis did not work out as planned. Instead of red, Diesbach made blue.\n\nThe oil had reacted to produce cyanide, a vital ingredient of Prussian blue. Diesbach kept his recipe secret for many years, but it was discovered and published in 1724, and after that anyone could make the colour. By the 1750s it cost just a tenth as much as ultramarine. But it was not such a glorious blue, and painters were still not satisfied. They got a better alternative in 1802, when the French chemist Louis Jacques Thénard invented cobalt blue.\n\nBest of all was the discovery in 1826 of a way of making ultramarine itself. In 1824 the French Society for the Encouragement of National Industry had offered a prize of 6,000 francs to anyone who could make artificial ultramarine at an affordable price.\n\nThe Toulouse chemist Jean-Baptiste Guimet won the prize two years later, when he showed that ultramarine could be made by heating china clay, soda, charcoal, sand and sulphur in a furnace. This meant there was no longer any need to rely on the scarce natural source, and ultramarine eventually became a fairly cheap commercial pigment (called French ultramarine, because it was first mass-produced in Paris).\n\nIn the 1950s synthetic ultramarine became the source of what is claimed to be the most beautiful blue in the world. Invented by the French artist Yves Klein together with a Parisian paint manufacturer, Edouard Adam, International Klein Blue is a triumph of modern chemistry. Klein was troubled by the way pigments lost their richness when they were mixed with a liquid binder to make paint. With Adam's help, he found that a synthetic resin, thinned with organic solvents, would keep this vibrant texture in the dry paint layer. In 1957 Klein launched his new blue with a series of single-colour paintings, and in 1960 he protected his invention with a patent."
